# Commit documentation

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:55:25Z

## Purpose and applicability

Make future commits understandable to someone reviewing repository history.
These conventions do not require revising existing commits.

Coursework follows its applicable course instructions.

## Subject

Use a short, specific, imperative summary describing the change.
Aim for about 72 characters without sacrificing clarity.

Use a scope label when it helps identify the affected component.
Scope labels and a particular commit-message syntax are not mandatory.

Avoid vague subjects such as "update files" or "fix things."

## Body

For substantive changes, explain:

- The problem or purpose.
- Significant behavior changes.
- Verification performed and relevant limitations.

Include assumptions, risks, or invariants when they help explain or assess
the particular change. Do not require a fixed set of fields for every commit.

Link to relevant ADRs or repository documents for fuller reasoning rather than
copying debriefs or decision records into the message.

Small, obvious changes can use the subject alone.

Follow [documentation_debrief.mdc](documentation_debrief.mdc) when important
reasoning remains undocumented, and [testing.mdc](testing.mdc) for accurate
verification reporting.

## Commit boundaries

Keep each commit coherent and independently understandable.
Group related implementation, tests, and documentation when they form one change.

Before an authorized commit, inspect the intended diff and staged contents.
Include only the agreed work. Do not stage unrelated changes or all repository
contents indiscriminately.

If staged changes include work outside the agreed scope, identify it and agree
on how to proceed without silently changing another person's staging choices.

## Authorization and history

Drafting a commit message does not authorize creating a commit.
Authorization to commit does not authorize pushing, merging, amending existing
commits, or rewriting history.

Apply these conventions to future commits. Do not rewrite existing commit
history to conform to them.

Follow [coauthoring_protocol.mdc](coauthoring_protocol.mdc) for ownership,
scope, and human review.

## Related documentation

Follow [documentation_core.mdc](documentation_core.mdc) for authoritative
documentation locations and [documentation_decisions.mdc](documentation_decisions.mdc)
for decision-to-commit references.

Changelog practices belong in [changelog.mdc](changelog.mdc).
